#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:6
msgid "Handling Errors"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:8
msgid ""
"Because procedural macros execute during compilation, standard runtime error "
"reporting strategies do not apply. If a macro receives malformed input, it "
"must signal the error to the compiler."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:12
msgid "There are two main ways to report errors in procedural macros:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:14
msgid "1. Panicking"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:16
msgid ""
"If your macro code panics, the compiler will catch the panic, halt "
"compilation, and print the panic message as a compiler error:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:20
msgid "\"Invalid struct name\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:23
msgid ""
"This may occur by default if there are any bugs or programming errors in "
"your procedural macro."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:26
msgid "2. Emitting `compile_error!` (Preferred)"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:28
msgid ""
"A more friendly approach is to return a `TokenStream` containing a call to "
"the built-in `compile_error!` macro. This allows the compiler to highlight "
"the exact span where the error occurred:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:33
msgid "// Returning an explicit compile error TokenStream:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:34
msgid "\"Expected a valid identifier\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:35
msgid "// Generates compile_error!(\"...\")\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:42
msgid ""
"Explain that panicking is easy but has a terrible developer experience. It "
"halts the entire compilation and doesn't tell the developer where they made "
"a mistake (the error is tracked to the macro definition site rather than the "
"particular call site)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/errors.md:46
msgid ""
"Emphasize that emitting `compile_error!` is the standard, idiomatic way to "
"handle user input validation. The error message is attached to a specific "
"`Span` (the location of the bad token), which allows IDEs and compiler error "
"messages to draw a red squiggly line directly under the offending code."
msgstr ""
